8. “Why Might This Be?” Contextual Hypotheses
Data reveals what patterns exist, but historical and cultural context helps us understand why those visual conventions may have developed. Below, we strictly distinguish verified mathematical findings from cultural hypotheses.


&lt;div&gt;
  &lt;div class="why-data-callout"&gt;
    &lt;div class="why-data-title"&gt;&lt;i class="bi bi-graph-up"&gt;&lt;/i&gt; What the Data Shows&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;p style="margin: 0.4rem 0 0; color: #e0f7fa;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Metal&lt;/strong&gt; has the lowest average brightness of all genres (0.342) and devotes &lt;strong&gt;44.6%&lt;/strong&gt; of cover pixels to deep shadows, compared to the 30.1% dataset average.&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="why-reason-callout"&gt;
    &lt;div class="why-reason-title"&gt;&lt;i class="bi bi-chat-left-quote"&gt;&lt;/i&gt; Possible Reasons&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;p style="margin: 0.4rem 0 0; color: #f3e5f5;"&gt;Heavy metal themes often center on darkness, nocturnal lore, dread, and rebellious counterculture. Early album designers in the 1970s and 80s (e.g. Black Sabbath, Iron Maiden) established high-contrast chiaroscuro and midnight backgrounds as genre emblems.&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;


&lt;div&gt;
  &lt;div class="why-data-callout"&gt;
    &lt;div class="why-data-title"&gt;&lt;i class="bi bi-graph-up"&gt;&lt;/i&gt; What the Data Shows&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;p style="margin: 0.4rem 0 0; color: #e0f7fa;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Disco&lt;/strong&gt; exhibits the highest saturation of any genre (&lt;strong&gt;0.504&lt;/strong&gt;), towering over rock (0.291) and jazz (0.416).&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="why-reason-callout"&gt;
    &lt;div class="why-reason-title"&gt;&lt;i class="bi bi-chat-left-quote"&gt;&lt;/i&gt; Possible Reasons&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;p style="margin: 0.4rem 0 0; color: #f3e5f5;"&gt;Disco emerged in 1970s dance club culture driven by strobe lights, neon fixtures, glitter, and vibrant fashion. Album covers mirrored the physical interior environment of the discotheque.&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;


&lt;div&gt;
  &lt;div class="why-data-callout"&gt;
    &lt;div class="why-data-title"&gt;&lt;i class="bi bi-graph-up"&gt;&lt;/i&gt; What the Data Shows&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;p style="margin: 0.4rem 0 0; color: #e0f7fa;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Techno&lt;/strong&gt; has the lowest mean saturation (&lt;strong&gt;0.274&lt;/strong&gt;), with over 55% of albums featuring grey, white, or black dominant palettes.&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="why-reason-callout"&gt;
    &lt;div class="why-reason-title"&gt;&lt;i class="bi bi-chat-left-quote"&gt;&lt;/i&gt; Possible Reasons&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;p style="margin: 0.4rem 0 0; color: #f3e5f5;"&gt;Techno aesthetics emerged from Detroit and Berlin club undergrounds, inspired by industrial machinery, brutalist warehouse spaces, and white-label vinyl test pressings that deliberately rejected glossy commercial pop marketing.&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;


&lt;div&gt;
  &lt;div class="why-data-callout"&gt;
    &lt;div class="why-data-title"&gt;&lt;i class="bi bi-graph-up"&gt;&lt;/i&gt; What the Data Shows&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;p style="margin: 0.4rem 0 0; color: #e0f7fa;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Reggae&lt;/strong&gt; has the highest warm tone ratio (&lt;strong&gt;0.522&lt;/strong&gt;) of any genre—over 52% of all pixels skew red, gold, or warm earth tones.&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="why-reason-callout"&gt;
    &lt;div class="why-reason-title"&gt;&lt;i class="bi bi-chat-left-quote"&gt;&lt;/i&gt; Possible Reasons&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;p style="margin: 0.4rem 0 0; color: #f3e5f5;"&gt;Reggae visual culture is intimately tied to Pan-African and Rastafarian symbolism, utilizing red, gold, green, and black, alongside tropical Caribbean lighting and Jamaican landscape photography.&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

